WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:07.830
Everyone makes mistakes, even in a galaxy
far, far away.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:14.160
Not even Jedi masters, Sith lords, or George
Lucas are free from the occasional blunder.

00:00:14.160 --> 00:00:19.250
Star Wars ushered in a new era of digital
special effects and filmmaking, but the filmmakers

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:21.580
behind it all are all still human.

00:00:21.580 --> 00:00:26.800
If you look beyond the lightsabers, it's apparent
in the mistakes and missteps you can find

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:29.150
in each of the movies in the saga.

00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:34.540
And that's not even counting Jar Jar Binks,
nature’s biggest mistake.

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:37.280
“It’s embarrassing.”

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:38.420
Keira who?

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:42.130
There's a reason people forget Keira Knightley
was in The Phantom Menace.

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:48.110
The British actress plays Sabé, a quiet handmaiden
who serves as the costumed decoy of Natalie

00:00:48.110 --> 00:00:50.960
Portman’s Queen Amidala throughout most
of the movie.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:56.240
Being a fake-out queen isn’t much of a role,
but the fact that Knightley's first name is

00:00:56.240 --> 00:01:00.170
misspelled in the end credits probably doesn't
help either.

00:01:00.170 --> 00:01:02.620
Those handmaidens don’t have very good luck.

00:01:02.620 --> 00:01:06.010
“Padme, clean this droids up as best you
can.”

00:01:06.010 --> 00:01:07.010
Head of the class

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:11.020
Although the Jedi younglings that Yoda trains
in Attack of the Clones are capable of some

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:16.170
astounding things, one appears to be just
a little more capable than the rest.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:20.100
The little guy with the reptilian face who
starts the shot out near the middle of the

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:24.229
group is suddenly behind everyone after an
angle change, and then at the head of the

00:01:24.229 --> 00:01:25.820
class after a quick cut.

00:01:25.820 --> 00:01:30.640
It's probably just a continuity error, but
it could also be a pretty solid Jedi trick.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:32.350
We’ll never find out.

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:33.350
Thanks, Anakin.

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:35.460
The disappearing jacket

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:39.119
Who knows what weird stuff you might find
inside a Jedi's home?

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:44.180
When Luke Skywalker swings by Obi-Wan Kenobi's
hut to try out his dad's lightsaber in A New

00:01:44.180 --> 00:01:47.119
Hope, there's initially a jacket hanging from
the archway.

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:49.999
“This is a weapon of a Jedi knight.”

00:01:49.999 --> 00:01:52.179
But after a cut, it's nowhere to be seen.

00:01:52.179 --> 00:01:53.179
Did you miss it?

00:01:53.179 --> 00:01:57.350
Must have gone to the dark side… or maybe
just the laundry.

00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.889
The vanishing stormtrooper

00:01:59.889 --> 00:02:06.319
In A New Hope, Obi-Wan Kenobi uses the Force
to convince stormtroopers that C-3PO and R2-D2

00:02:06.319 --> 00:02:08.410
aren't the droids they're looking for.

00:02:08.410 --> 00:02:10.800
“These aren’t the droids you are looking
for.”

00:02:10.800 --> 00:02:13.110
But apparently, he could've just made them
disappear.

00:02:13.110 --> 00:02:17.410
After a quick cut, the stormtrooper who was
right behind them suddenly vanishes.

00:02:17.410 --> 00:02:20.570
Jedi trick, or impromptu lunch break?

00:02:20.570 --> 00:02:22.080
Vader's quick change

00:02:22.080 --> 00:02:27.330
During Darth Vader and Obi-Wan Kenobi’s
epic lightsaber battle at the end of A New

00:02:27.330 --> 00:02:32.850
Hope, Vader gets pretty mixed up, and not
just because Obi-Wan evaporates into his own

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:33.340
robe.

00:02:33.340 --> 00:02:34.340
“Nooo”

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.680
The buttons on Vader’s chest plate first
appear on the right, then they switch to his

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:39.680
left.

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:43.190
If only switching between the light and dark
sides were so easy.

00:02:43.190 --> 00:02:44.810
Model misbehavior

00:02:44.810 --> 00:02:49.820
The battle sequences on Hoth in The Empire
Strikes Back were larger than life, especially

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:53.360
because of the Empire's enormous Imperial
Walkers.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:58.040
But looking back, some of the practical special
effects seem a little crude.

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.680
After Luke blows up the guts of one of the
massive war machines with a grenade, it comes

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:04.250
tumbling down.

00:03:04.250 --> 00:03:05.250
Watch the back leg.

00:03:05.250 --> 00:03:10.370
You can't miss the pole used to tip over the
miniature model, which comes out of the ground

00:03:10.370 --> 00:03:11.680
and quickly retracts.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.390
Not so slick.

00:03:13.390 --> 00:03:14.930
Millennium flyby

00:03:14.930 --> 00:03:19.840
George Lucas took special effects light years
into the future, but his team at Industrial

00:03:19.840 --> 00:03:22.230
Light &amp; Magic were learning on the job.

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:26.840
To depict asteroids, they used everything
from old shoes to potatoes.

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:31.110
In a view from the helm of the Millennium
Falcon during The Empire Strikes Back, you

00:03:31.110 --> 00:03:34.830
can briefly see the Millennium Falcon fly
past itself.

00:03:34.830 --> 00:03:40.220
It’s blurry, but that’s Han’s ship doing
a barrel roll from an unused scene.

00:03:40.220 --> 00:03:41.710
That’s some impressive flying.

00:03:41.710 --> 00:03:43.040
Crazy carbonite

00:03:43.040 --> 00:03:48.620
Being frozen alive in a block of carbonite
can't be a ton of fun, but Han Solo seems

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:50.940
to have found a way to let loose.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:55.760
His arms are clearly bound behind his back
when he’s lowered into the freezing chamber

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:57.540
in The Empire Strikes Back.

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:02.470
But when he's set free in Return of the Jedi,
Solo's arms are somehow unshackled.

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:04.980
What really happened in there?

00:04:04.980 --> 00:04:06.200
Lando flips

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:11.010
Lando Calrissian borrows a perplexing move
from Darth Vader during his appearance in

00:04:11.010 --> 00:04:12.190
Return of the Jedi.

00:04:12.190 --> 00:04:18.650
As he playfully calls his pal Han Solo a "pirate,"
Calrissian is suddenly shown wearing all his

00:04:18.650 --> 00:04:20.930
clothes the opposite way.

00:04:20.930 --> 00:04:22.620
Rey makes her point

00:04:22.620 --> 00:04:27.169
In The Force Awakens, it's pretty clear that
Rey is far from an ordinary young woman, but

00:04:27.169 --> 00:04:31.870
at one point, she does something pretty extraordinary
on board the Millennium Falcon.

00:04:31.870 --> 00:04:35.939
As she and Finn are repairing the ship, she
points at something with her right hand in

00:04:35.939 --> 00:04:36.939
a close-up.

00:04:36.939 --> 00:04:39.909
The wide shot shows her pointing with her
left.

00:04:39.909 --> 00:04:40.909
Thanks for watching!

00:04:40.909 --> 00:04:44.729
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:04:44.729 --> 00:04:48.059
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Star Wars mistakes you’ve caught...

